00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.939 align:start position:0%
 
that very question to the former Foreign  Secretary David Miliband who's no  president and chief executive of the  International Rescue Committee I think  you're absolutely right to say that the  test of these summits is not the words  but the actions the UN summit happening  tomorrow monday is at the moment plagued  by political division between different  countries and that's put the onus on a  second summit on Tuesday that President  Obama has called we're practical  concrete steps need to be taken first of  all to increase the amount and  effectiveness of international  humanitarian aid secondly to raise the  number of refugees the most vulnerable  widows and orphans who are resettled  into richer countries and thirdly to  take on the people smugglers who have  made such profit out of human misery  over the last year or two well the  European Union talk of and we we know  that the pressures that have been put on  so many members of that union by  refugees talk of a global shared  responsibility do you see then coming  out of this global quotas I think that  it's premature to talk about that  because eighty-five percent of the  world's refugees are in poor countries  not in rich countries it's countries  like Kenya Pakistan middle-income  country like Turkey has over two million  refugees a country like Lebanon has over  one and a half million refugees what I  think we do need is a clear commitment  from the richer countries that the  smaller number of vulnerable less than  ten percent probably round five percent  of the most vulnerable victims of war  need to be resettled in countries like  the US the UK across europe etc we  actually need the gulf and other  countries to participate as well but  there is an obvious need not just to  deal with the symptoms of the problem  that's the fleeing refugees but also to  deal with the humanitarian conditions in  countries like Lebanon Jordan Pakistan  that are driving people to seek a refuge  in Europe in the first place so what  does that mean for the United Kingdom at  the moment twenty thousand or so  refugees over five years that doesn't  sound like a lot you're right to say  that the UK commitment is very small  what I always explain to people is that  the current government's commitment to  Syrian refugees the 20,000 over five  years that you referred to is the  equivalent of six refugees /  parliamentary constituency you know that  I used to represent south shields in  Parliament there's no way anyone can  persuade me that six refugees is going  to overwhelm the 65,000 people who are  residents of South Shields and I think  there's quite a lot of scope for Theresa  May to come to the UN and to the Obama  summit on Tuesday and say look the UK  has a really good record on  international overseas aid we're a  leader the UK is a leader through the  department for international development  on global humanitarian help but we can  also make a greater contribution beyond  the six refugees / parliamentary  constituency frankly the UK should be  saying will will take 20 or 25,000 a  year so four times the current level 25  refugees / parliamentary constituency  rather than just six because countries  like Canada are already doing that the  Canadian government came in last year I  was meeting them just this week they  committed to take 25,000 people it's a  country of half the population of the UK  so I think the UK could do more on the  refugee resettlement side to match the  frankly exemplary performance that the  UK has on international humanitarian aid  and let's focus on Syria now and first  of all in relation to I suppose the  first question I asked you about the  United Nations and how effective it can  be or has been in trying to sort it out  there doesn't it tell us everything  about the UN that it's the United States  and Russia who by and large have  brokered this ceasefire well my  organization has 2,000 staff inside  Syria we had five people killed in an  acid or russian bombing raid about a  month ago who are running a hospital in  der I in the southwest of the country  the reports I'm getting from the ground  are that the ceasefire is more or less  holding there's localized outbreaks of  violence but the truth about the UN  which speaks to the broader question  you're asking is that it's only as  strong as the strong member states  within it and how they work together so  when Russia and America are divided as  they have been on the Syria issue over  the last five years the UN becomes  paralyzed  and we've seen the effects of that with  the 500,000 killed the 5 million  refugees the 7 million internally  displaced and Syria is a poster child  for what happens when the great powers  are divided on the big issues of the day  and the fact that the US and Russia are  finally working together is only the  beginning because the truth is that all  of us know that a short term ceasefire  needs to be complemented by a serious  political process that addresses the  fundamental problem of Syria which is  that the Assad government is  illegitimate in the eyes of the majority  of the people and that's something that  needs to be addressed so well you know  the next question then where might it  lead it's a big if of course in the  early days of this ceasefire but in  terms of a more lasting settlement the  calms things down in which people can  hopefully return to the country does it  require the removal of President Assad I  think that the critical issue is that  while the current government which has  been responsible for the majority of the  deaths has ultimate power then there is  going to be an ongoing civil conflict of  an absolutely fundamental kind and the  issue that needs to be addressed is what  the so-called transition the the  original agreement in 2012 in Geneva  talked about a transition from the  current government and the big question  is what's it a transition to the  Russians have always argued it's going  to be a transition to chaos and there  hasn't been a proper denunciation of  what the new power structure will be  various proposals have been made by  civil society groups but from our  position as a humanitarian organization  it's not our job to choose the  government is our job to say that the  civilians deserve a government that will  respect their rights and one which both  the great powers and the regional powers  are willing to expect respect let me ask  you for your political analysis of the  effects of the refugee crisis do you see  it as being linked to the rise of  nationalist anti-immigration parties I  think there's no question that the fear  among many voters that borders are not  controlled has contributed to a rise of  what I would call the polarization  around the politics of refugees and  immigration secondly I think it's also  the case that the confusion between a  refugee who is fleeing for his or her  life and an immigrant who is fleeing for  a better life it's not the one is good  and the other is bad it's just that  different the confusion of those two  categories has also made the politics  very difficult and thirdly I think it's  reasonable to point out that in the wake  of the financial crisis the straightened  economic circumstances of Western  countries has contributed to a sense of  disenfranchisement and disenchantment  from my point of view the absolutely  essential thing is that we preserve the  fundamental fact that riff refugees are  fleeing for their lives you only take to  the boats of the Mediterranean in  absolute desperation and do you see then  you see a dollop of those sentiments of  alienation presumably in the brexit vote  in the UK do you think that that  ultimately will have to come to a a  second vote even if it's just about the  shape and form of the brexit settlement  I think the truth is that not only does  the government of the UK not know how  the brig zyk negotiation is going to be  conducted or even what they're  negotiating platform is going to be but  none of us know how long this is going  to take or what the eventual outcome is  going to be people have clearly voted by  four percent to leave the European Union  that has to be respected the government  has to take forward its commitments in  that regard and I think for people like  me who argued very strongly including on  your program for Britain to remain a  member of the European Union we've got  to respect the result it would be a dead  end to start for me to start saying how  this is ultimately going to end up what  I do think i should say is that there  need to be some very clear negotiating  tests for the government the government  had a responsibility to have a  negotiation that does maximize the  economic opportunity for britain both in  terms of the market for goods and  services but also in terms of inward  investment it does have to address the  immigration issues that have been raised  in the course of the campaign which were  clearly part of the result and Mr  Miliband let's ask you about the  American political scene we know how  closely you and Hillary Clinton worked  together while she was Secretary of  State newer Foreign Secretary are you  offering her any advice on her current  campaign that's charming of you to  suggest that I should do so but she's  got plenty of advisors and most  important she's got a very clear idea of  her own about the campaign that she  wants to run and about the way in which  she wants to lead at the country I mean  I can tell you what I'm sure you've  covered which is that this is a  tumultuous campaign the only rule is  that there are no rules at all and i  think that the election is going to come  into focus over the next three or four  weeks one thing that I tell people here  that is different from the brexit  referendum is that in the brig zyk case  the status quo was quite well known and  in many ways not liked but the  alternative Briggs it was completely  unknown that was the referendum in an  election campaign especially one like  the one in the US at the moment both  sides are very well known both mrs.  Clinton and Donald Trump have been  fixtures of the national scene for 25  years and so this is very different from  a brexit situation it's a competition  between two very well known quantities  and I think that will in the end be  important in the final decision that  people make speculation about jobs  concern you Mr Miliband if you are  retaining your British citizenship is a  return to the Labour Party is that off  the agenda as well yeah I'm focused on  my job here in the US I've just just  finished my foot my three years here  obviously it grieves people like me that  the Labour Party should be so far from  power a country like the UK desperately  needs I mean any advanced industrialized  democracy frankly desperately needs a  credible committed alternative  government and that's not just good for  democracy it's actually essential for  the functioning of the political system  and at the moment the UK voter doesn't  have that kind  option we'll see what happens in the  labor election that's coming up but  there is a fundamental argument that  needs to be had in center left parties  and it's not just about the tactics to  win it's about the values that we stand  for and the way in which we take forward  those values and radically change  circumstances and the argument with  jeremy corbyn is not just about his  electoral tactics it's about the fact  that many of us think he's wrong on on  key issues whether it be opening coal  mines reopening coal mines or Britain's  role in NATO mr miliband great talking  to you thank you so much David Miliband  thank you very much indeed

